Oh, yes, there are other ways to put a clock on the player.  However,
in an environment so patently unrealistic as Nethack (when it comes down
to it, you are walking into a hole in the ground full of sentient humanoids
that don't need to eat), aging seems more or less impossible to implement 
cleanly.  Also, once you implement aging, people start asking all kinds
of awkward questions like "how old is old?", "how long is a turn?" and "if
we're having realism, why can 473 soldier corpses, their assorted armour
and weapons (which fell off their bodies the instant they died), and a
black dragon occupy a doorway that can't accommodate two newts?"

I've only played one roguelike - ADOM - that implements aging at all
meaningfully, and it's mostly only relevant if you get attacked by
monsters that cause magical aging.  It also takes a metric buttload of
*other* measures to prevent the player farming monsters, some more
successful than others.  (Including the one you hate, of making monsters
more dangerous as the player kills more of them.  However, ADOM does
that in, frankly, a completely insane fashion.)

Crawl's clock is very simple: monsters don't get generated fast enough
to keep the player fed if they hang around for long periods on cleared
levels.

Omega... it's too long since I've played Omega.  Pass.

Angband... the only "clock" on the player is their own boredom
threshold.

The rest?  Buggered if I know.

